hen you come across Catherine and you  find her in a similar State though she  is aware of her cyborg properties and  seems okay with it even more she points  out that Simon is in the same boat have  you looked at yourself lately you're a  walking talking diving suit with some  Electronics left on for good measure I  don't you don't want to think about it  we'll start thinking about it Catherine  explains what's going on as a final  measure against the end of the world  Humanity would be preserved digitally  through her project here at pathos 2. I  say saved all the people on the station  as brain scans and put them into an  artificial world  we were going to launch it into space to  save it from uh well all of this you  find out that the brain scan from the  beginning of the game copied your  Consciousness which was then uploaded  into your diving suit body to start the  game the original Flesh and Blood Simon  died decades ago but importantly this  process was a copy and paste not a cut  and paste the original Simon continued  living his life after the scan he never  had any experience in pathos 2. the game  has us undergo this copy and paste two  more times and each time it is a pivotal  moment in the story The two additional  transfers that happen at pathos 2 both  involve putting Simon's Consciousness in  a new body the first time is because he  needs a stronger suit to go deeper  underwater and the second time is the  final uploading of his Consciousness  onto the artificial World Catherine made  called The Arc as it heads to space the  second moment is basically the end of  the game and is probably what most  players will chiefly remember it has a  lot of emotional weight but I would  argue that the first transfer is at  least as if not more important in the  latter half of the game Simon needs to  go deeper into the water to complete his  mission of sending the ark to space and  this requires a stronger suit to  withstand the increased pressure so you  follow Catherine's guidance and prepare  the transfer she copies your  Consciousness and puts it into the new  suit but then something goes wrong you  as Simon wake up in the new body but the  old Simon can be heard muttering just a  few words before Catherine forcefully  sedates him  foreign  this breaks something in the mind of  Simon the one in the second suit  two Simons there can't be two Simons  even though he had it laid out before  Simon never realized or let himself own  up to the fact that the process was copy  and paste not cut and paste not anything  like a brain transplant as he says he  always thought there was just the one  him continuing through life when he  realizes that's not true that there are  multiple Simons he loses it he lashes  out a Catherine saying how did you  expect me to react to this [ __ ] please  stop you're [ __ ] disgusting  Simon thought he was always himself just  shifting from one body to another in  reality it would probably be fairer to  say that he was being cloned each copy  and paste creating another Simon to  experience reality for the most part the  game only follows the conscious  experience of one of these Simons except  for this small moment were two overlap  however briefly the game has this whole  experience of going through the scans  not realizing what was going on  retroactively being shown that you are  not who you thought you were all of that  Simon and the player go through together  as one unit and here the player and  Simon are confronted together there are  two Simons but only one of them is us  the player and only one feels real to  the second suit Simon this kind of story  and critical moment in a movie or book  or any other medium wouldn't work the  same in any other medium the person  experiencing the work wouldn't be able  to go back and stare at the player model  the body that they inhabited up to the  point but that's exactly what we get to  do with Simon yes maybe Simon reacts  more harshly than the player might feel  but overall I found the coalescence of  the player and Simon in this moment to  be an almost perfect mesh between game  and player Simon's confusion over who he  is and what has happened occurs in line  with our confusion over taking up this  new body and realizing that from the  first brain scan our sense of self was  wrong plus there's the decision of what  to do with the old diving suit Simon too  to let him wake up and live alone in  this section of the station or to pull  the plug drain his battery and leave  only one Simon still alive the sequence  and the whole game really use these  themes of body and mind really well to  make us question our personal identity  an important philosophical topic who is  the real Simon throughout the game does  a twice copied clone get to think of  themselves as being identical with the  original human and what makes Simon  Simon after each copy and paste these  questions are what Simon and we as the  player have to Grapple with to me in  contrast with the name of the game the  body is the less important part as each  Simon has the same Consciousness at  least at the point of the copying they  are all still Simon though once their  life experiences diverge they each begin  to take on their own new identity  the beautiful thing is that this  exploration of personal identity gets to  be so visceral and real because Soma is  a video game and not any other kind of  media we play in first person as Simon  we get to feel and act as him in a way  more real than with films or books great  works in these other media May evoke  sympathy or feelings of likeness in good  cases they let us relate to the  characters but they do not let the  person experiencing them be the depicted  subject like a game does there is a  game's philosopher see the Nguyen who I  cited in my Eldon ring philosophy video  and he captures this unique aspect of  games masterfully in his book games  agency as art in the work nguin charts  how games occupy a distinct artistic  space because they use the medium of  agency games let us take on a temporary  agency a way of being or an attitude or  headspace with specific desires or goals  as new in writes games turn out to be a  way of writing down forms of agency of  inscribing them in in an artifact games  are one of our techniques where  inscribing and recording Bits Of Human  Experience instead of recording a  beautiful sound a stunning image or an  evocative turn of phrase games record  Human Experience a way of being or  feeling or thinking and doing for  example a game like twister records and  presents a fun goofy agency when you  play it you generally take on a  light-hearted attitude on the other hand  a game like chess gives a focused  tactical mindset generally a much more  serious attitude and circling back a  game like Soma delivers the agency of  Simon Jarrett actually a few Simon  jarretts as they grapple with the  horrors of pathos 2 and what it means to  be a person what it means to be  themselves I could also talk about the  final copy and paste in the game the  decisive moments where big spoiler alert  here Bjork is launched into space but we  as players remain with the second suit  Simon and pathos 2. here we get to see  the other side of the agency coin flip  where we are the ones left behind  this fleshes out the copy paste idea and  it serves as a strong emotional note  that stays with the player of second  suit Simon's desperation but still I  think everything we really need to look  at is in that first moment the second is  a great addition to the game but the  first can stand on its own to show us  what kind of medium video games are and  can be game designers and games in the  future would do well to incorporate this  kind of use of the medium these are  games not books or movies or anything  else and they can Shine for that reason  and they are video games too so of  course keep throwing in all the  beautiful landscapes and soundscapes for